30 ABSTRACT
31 BACKGROUND: Wastes can be used as rearing substrate by black soldier fly (BSF)
32 larvae, the latter being exploitable as protein source in animal feed. This research
33 aimed to assess the influence of four rearing substrates [Trial 1 (organic wastes): a
34 mixture of vegetable and fruit (VEGFRU) vs a mixture of fruits only (FRU); Trial 2
35 (agro-industrial by-products): brewery (BRE) vs winery (WIN) by-products] on BSF
36 larvae development, waste reduction efficiency, and nutritional composition.
37 RESULTS: If respectively compared to FRU and WIN, VEGFRU and BRE larvae
38 needed less time to reach the prepupae stage (22.0, 22.2, 20.2 and 8.0 days of
39 trial, respectively) and had higher protein content (229.7, 257.3, 312.9 and 395.7
40 g kg-1 DM). The waste reduction index ranged from 2.4 (WIN) to 5.3 g d-1 (BRE).
41 BRE larvae showed the lowest saturated and the highest polyunsaturated fatty
42 acids proportions (612.4 and 260.1 g kg-1 total fatty acids, respectively).
43 CONCLUSION: Vegetable and fruit wastes and winery by-products can be used as
44 rearing substrates for BSF larvae mass production. Brewery by-products led to very
45 promising larvae performances and nutritional composition. However, given BRE
46 limited availability, low BRE dietary inclusion levels could be used with the purpose
47 of increasing larvae performances.
