314 The need for high dietary moisture content could be ascribed to the morphology of
315 the mouthparts of BSF larvae, which resembles the characteristics of scavenger
316 insects.28,29 This kind of macerating mouth apparatus allows BSF larvae to scrape
317 off the food from the feeding surface. By softening the feed solids, increased
dietary moisture content makes easier for the larvae to feed.30
318
319 The results obtained in our trials could be also reflective of possible differences
320 between rearing substrates in terms of the content of nutrients other than CP (e.g.,
321 ether extract, structural and non-structural carbohydrates, amino acids) and/or in
322 terms of nutrient digestibility. In both trials, the EE content of substrates (<90 g
323 kg-1 DM) was far below the 200-260 g kg-1 found by Nguyen et al.12,13 to have
324 detrimental effects for the survival of BSF larvae and adults. BRE larvae showed
325 very good performances despite the high structural carbohydrates content of the
326 relative rearing substrate (NDF: 447 g kg-1 DM; ADF: 225 g kg-1 DM). Such a result
327 clearly demonstrates that BSF larvae are also able to efficiently bioconvert wastes
328 and by-products characterized by high fiber content, thanks to the presence, in the
329 digestive tract of the insect, of intestinal bacteria able to degrade cellulose.31 The
330 amino acid composition of the rearing substrates was not analyzed in our trials, and
331 little literature is available concerning the effects of dietary amino acids on
332 development and nutritional composition of BSF larvae.32,33 Studying the nutritional
333 composition of BSF prepupae reared on different organic waste substrates,
334 Spranghers et al.32 showed that the amino acid content of the prepupae had narrow
335 ranges, particularly when compared to the noticeable differences found in the
336 amino acid composition of the rearing substrates. Concerning nutrient digestibility,
337 to the best of our knowledge no studies are currently available. Further studies are
338 necessary to deepen these aspects for the optimization of BSF feeding and
339 nutrition.
