402 C18:1 c9 was the main represented MUFA in the larvae, while C18:2 n6 and C18:3
403 n3 were the main represented PUFA n6 and PUFA n3, respectively. The low quantity
404 of recovered n3 PUFA in the larvae could represent a problem if insect meals are
405 intended to be used for animal feed. Indeed, researches highlighted a decrease in
406 nutritional product quality with the inclusion of insect meals in animal diets
407 especially when full-fat meals are used.5,6,46 Nevertheless, BSF larvae can be
408 enriched in n3-PUFA through the substrate.33,47 Authors 10,48 reported that C12:0 is
409 a good inhibitor of bacteria strains and could be of great interest in the reduction of
410 the use of antibiotics in animal feeding.10,49,50 In this context, BSF larvae reared on
411 organic wastes resulted very interesting with up to 574 g kg-1 TFA of lauric acid.
412
413 Especially in Southern Europe, the large availability of vegetable and fruit wastes
414 (mainly from markets and supermarkets) may allow the development of a BSF
415 larvae mass production, enabling as well to obtain economic and environmental
416 benefits from the sustainable management of organic wastes. Regarding the
417 considered agro-industrial by-products, the use of winery by-products as rearing
418 substrate for BSF larvae could be conditioned, both from a technological and
419 economical point of view, by the need of preliminarily processing to remove or
420 reduce polyphenols, pesticides and insecticides contents, which could exert a
421 negative influence on the growth performance of the larvae. Remarkable positive
422 results were obtained in terms of overall development time, growth performances
423 and nutritional composition for the larvae reared on brewery by-products, which
424 should therefore be considered promising rearing substrates. However, as brewery
425 by-products are characterized by a more limited availability than vegetables, fruits
