428 Overall, our results show that the performance and chemical composition of BSF
429 larvae are largely affected by the chemical composition of the provided substrate.
430 This clearly demonstrates that insects, like farm animals, have nutritional
431 requirements which have to be met for optimal performance.
432 The performances obtained in our bench top trials may vary when transferred to an
433 industrial scale. For instance, the large volumes of waste used as well as the high
434 larvae concentration could result in environmental oxygen depletion and heat
435 production.51 Attention should then be placed to the airflow to guarantee
436 appropriate rearing conditions. In addition, to optimize land use, insect breeding
437 should exploit the verticality of the breeding structure. However, this can lead to
438 the stratification of temperature and particular attention must be given to an
439 adequate air circulation to guarantee a homogeneous temperature in all parts of
440 the building. At industrial level, the production system would also require a
441 constant supply of substrates, possibly with a fairly constant chemical composition,
442 as to obtain BSF larvae with relatively constant nutrient profile.
443 Future studies should be designed to assess the nutritional requirements of BSF
444 larvae and to evaluate other agro-industrial by-products, as well as the effect of
445 mixing different organic wastes and agro-industrial by-products, to obtain optimal
446 BSF larvae performances in terms of development, waste reduction efficiency and
447 nutritional composition.
